WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.750
- [Kelly] Would You like some ice cream?

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:03.730
- First of all, very good job

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:04.668
on the Power of the Love.

00:00:04.668 --> 00:00:06.189
- Thank you!
(audience cheers)

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:07.469
Thank you.
- Very good.

00:00:07.469 --> 00:00:10.690
- It's hard to sing a
song that's so classic!

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:11.860
You know, when you hear that song,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.510
you think of your voice, no
matter who else sings it.

00:00:14.510 --> 00:00:16.120
So it's an amazing thing to,
("Power of Love")

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.510
oh you were watching!
(audience laughs)

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.763
I'm really glad, I'm really
happy I didn't know that.

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.019
But, did you write that
song for Back to the Future

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:26.060
back in the day?

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:27.030
- Yeah, sort of.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.160
I mean, when Amblin Entertainment asked

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.397
to have a meeting with us, and they said,

00:00:32.397 --> 00:00:34.267
"we've just written this film

00:00:34.267 --> 00:00:36.937
"and our lead character, Marty McFly,

00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:39.057
"his favorite band would
be Huey Lewis and the News.

00:00:39.057 --> 00:00:40.557
"So we thought, how would you like

00:00:40.557 --> 00:00:42.181
"to write a song for the film?"

00:00:42.181 --> 00:00:43.153
- That's amazing.
- And I thought,

00:00:44.093 --> 00:00:44.926
"Well, great, I'm flattered."

00:00:44.926 --> 00:00:46.730
I told them I'm flattered
but I didn't necessarily know

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:48.320
how to write for film.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:49.820
And I didn't fancy writing a song

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.502
called Back to the Future actually.

00:00:51.502 --> 00:00:52.335
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:52.335 --> 00:00:54.890
And they said, "No problem."
- It's a little on point.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.880
- Yeah, but they said they just wanted

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.180
one of your songs, do whatever you want.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.700
And so the next thing we
wrote was Power of Love.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.990
- But you were also in Back to the Future.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:06.090
You did a cameo, right?

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:07.350
- Oh, well, sort of, yeah.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:08.300
- That's amazing!

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.160
- They thought it would be fun, you know,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.700
if we did it kind of as a
uncredited disguise thing.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:15.533
- Perfect.
- Turned out great.

00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:18.050
- Yes, and what a killer song, oh my gosh.

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.563
Have you ever played in
Cincinnati, on the road?

00:01:21.571 --> 00:01:24.370
- Not more than 30 times.
(Kelly and audience laughs)

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:25.203
- I was gonna say, I was like,

00:01:25.203 --> 00:01:26.467
"That might be the dumbest question I've

00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.780
"ever asked an artist, yeah."

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.000
- Cincinnati's a great town.
- Yeah.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.100
- We played Riverbend a
bunch, we played all kinds

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:33.190
of places there, yeah.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.040
- I love all the audience,
being on the road,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:35.873
there's nothing like it.

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:37.780
- And you get to live
like a teenager, right?

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:38.680
- Yeah!

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.600
You get to sleep 'til noon.
- Food everywhere,

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.980
somebody does your laundry, it's great.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:44.650
- Yes, until it catches up with you

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:45.540
and you gain weight.

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.010
So you're working on a
Broadway musical, though,

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:49.630
in spite of the hearing
loss and everything, right?

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.590
- Yeah, we're very excited.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:54.070
We've been working on
it for about 10 years

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.580
and we put it up in San
Diego at the Old Globe.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.030
It's called the Heart of Rock and Roll

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:03.030
and we've just partnered with
Tony Award-winning producer

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.800
Hunter Arnold, who just
produced the hit Hadestown.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.990
- Yeah.
- And we're gonna bring it

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.170
to Broadway next season.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:12.310
- That's so cool!
(audience cheering)

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:13.972
I love Broadway.

00:02:13.972 --> 00:02:15.453
- I do too, I do too.

00:02:15.453 --> 00:02:18.219
- I love, I grew up in
theater, so I love it.

00:02:18.219 --> 00:02:19.052
Any kind of musical theater.
- And you know what else

00:02:19.052 --> 00:02:20.390
is great is the people, right?

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.930
- Yeah.
- They're so talented,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:26.900
smart, funny, and generous, and genuine.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.520
They're just great people to work with.

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:29.690
- Yeah, agreed, agreed.

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:31.320
A lot of my friends do
Broadway as well, now.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.020
But, despite your hearing loss, though,

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:34.550
you still put together the album, right?

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:35.770
Weather, right?

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:37.810
- Well, all that stuff was cut

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.710
before the two years ago.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:40.650
That was all in the can.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.397
- So it was only the seven--
- We were assembling,

00:02:42.397 --> 00:02:44.640
collecting, and writing.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.066
It took us, you know,
we're not very prolific.

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:48.665
(Kelly and audience chuckle)

00:02:48.665 --> 00:02:50.469
First of all, we were
doing 75 shows a year.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:52.120
- Yeah.
- Wow.

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.700
- So, that's busy, and with traveling,

00:02:53.700 --> 00:02:55.520
that's what, 120 shows?

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.686
So we're busy and we have lives and,

00:02:57.686 --> 00:03:01.400
as I said, we're not really
all that prolific, so.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.420
And songs, you know,
are just hard to write.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:06.440
I mean, especially when
you've written a few of them.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.230
But when we would get
an idea, we'd record it

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:10.760
and just put it in the can,

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.600
the theory being as long as we were good,

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.463
and my band, we were as
good as we ever were,

00:03:15.463 --> 00:03:17.701
then we would just wait
'til we had 10 songs,

00:03:17.701 --> 00:03:19.200
release the record.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.720
We had seven songs, my hearing collapsed,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:22.980
we waited kind of a year

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.060
to see if I could recover.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.070
No help, so we thought, let's release them

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:29.128
and share them with the fans.

00:03:29.128 --> 00:03:31.505
- Yeah and it's called Weather, yeah.

00:03:31.505 --> 00:03:32.650
(audience cheering)

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:36.160
So, what do you remember
from the earlier days

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:38.780
of being with Huey Lewis and
the News, the whole band.

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.420
What do you remember from
those days in the beginning?

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.358
- I remember being booed
off a lot of stages.

00:03:42.358 --> 00:03:45.957
- Shooed off! (laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:03:45.957 --> 00:03:47.482
- I'm serious!
- So were a lot of the greats.

00:03:47.482 --> 00:03:49.800
- No, our first tour, we opened

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.820
for the Doobie Brothers,
who were good friends

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.920
and great people, and they
really cared about us.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.260
And we pretty much got
booed off every stage,

00:03:57.260 --> 00:03:59.344
every night.
- What?

00:03:59.344 --> 00:04:02.329
We got so good at going from song to song,

00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:05.250
you know, without any silence.

00:04:05.250 --> 00:04:07.234
- Amen, don't give 'em room.
- Silence was deadly,

00:04:07.234 --> 00:04:08.530
that's it.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.210
And so, at the end of one song,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.960
we'd hold the sustain. (imitates chord)

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.990
And if the guitar played had to switch

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:15.960
for the next song, while we're holding

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.580
the sustain, he'd switch the guitar.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:18.540
(snaps)
(audience laughs)

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.140
One, two, three, four,
boom, next song! (laughs)

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:23.130
- Don't give those hecklers any moments,

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:24.480
yes, that's amazing.

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:26.440
That's horrible, who does that?

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.503
Just drunk, unlovable
people, that's who does that.

00:04:29.503 --> 00:04:31.730
That's mean.
(audience applauds)

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.260
I mean, I have had to listen

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.824
to some people sing that are popular

00:04:36.824 --> 00:04:39.597
and it's one of those
things where I'm like,

00:04:39.597 --> 00:04:41.184
"Booing would be applicable,"

00:04:41.184 --> 00:04:44.610
but you don't do it, that's rude!

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.150
- I tell ya, a funny story.

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.730
We were playing with the Doobie Brothers,

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:49.950
same thing, on that tour.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.940
We're in San Antonio, Texas,

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.510
which is a big town for the Doobies,

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.500
- They like alcohol.
- 'cause they wrote

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.160
China Grove and all that.
(audience laughs)

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.440
And now, and I'd never
been out in the hall.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.710
We were playing the big
arena, whatever it is,

00:05:02.710 --> 00:05:04.697
and I thought, "Tonight
I'm just gonna go out

00:05:04.697 --> 00:05:06.240
"and see what it's like."

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:07.610
'Cause, you know, the lights are down,

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:09.640
the Doobies are up, nobody's gonna know

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:11.200
who I am, I got a baseball hat.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.690
And plus we were just starting out,

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:14.320
nobody knew who we were anyway.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.002
So I walked to the very
back row of the arena

00:05:17.002 --> 00:05:19.890
and sit down, place was sold out.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.400
But in the very back row,
there were a few seats empty

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.030
because people had moved up.

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:27.550
And my nearest guy was maybe
where the end of that couch.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.550
There's a guy over there and it's dark.

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.796
And now, I suddenly realize,
as the Doobies are playing,

00:05:32.796 --> 00:05:34.730
oh they're at the spot where they're gonna

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.760
introduce themselves and how about a hand

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:38.813
for the opening act?

00:05:38.813 --> 00:05:40.677
And sure enough, and I said,

00:05:40.677 --> 00:05:41.980
"Oh, this'll be interesting".

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.417
And I sit there and Pat Simmons goes,

00:05:45.417 --> 00:05:46.927
"Well, hi everybody," you know,

00:05:46.927 --> 00:05:49.207
"We're the Doobie Brothers,
great to have you here.

00:05:49.207 --> 00:05:51.967
"And how about a big hand
for our opening act tonight,

00:05:51.967 --> 00:05:53.610
"Huey Lewis and the News?"

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.826
And this guys right here goes, "Boo!"

00:05:56.826 --> 00:05:58.800
(audience laughs)

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.863
Right here, right here.

00:06:02.845 --> 00:06:04.548
- Oh my gosh!
- I wanted to go

00:06:04.548 --> 00:06:05.980
over there and grab him.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:09.330
- That is an unlikable human!

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:11.040
That's so mean!

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.401
- He paid his money, he
can do whatever he wants.

00:06:13.401 --> 00:06:14.287
- Yeah, you know what, I woulda been like,

00:06:14.287 --> 00:06:16.618
"Still got your money!"
(audience laughs)

00:06:16.618 --> 00:06:18.360
That's what I (laughs).

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.543
Goin' to the bank!

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:24.083
Who's laughin' now?

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:27.913
That's a jerk, we hate him, okay.

